
<% content_for :title, "Play the Tape Forward | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="tape-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="tape-hero">
    <div class="tape-hero-content">

      <p class="tape-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / TOOLBOX / PLAY THE TAPE FORWARD
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>PLAY THE</span>
        <span>WHOLE</span>
        <span>TAPE.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="tape-intro">
        YOUR BRAIN LOVES THE HIGHLIGHTS.<br>
        MAKE IT WATCH THE WHOLE MOVIE.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- OPENING QUOTE -->
  <section class="tape-quote">
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        "Making a tape is like writing a letter —
        there's a lot of erasing and rethinking
        and starting again."
      </p>

      <cite>— Nick Hornby</cite>
    </blockquote>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / THE URGE -->
  <section class="tape-section tape-pink">
    <div class="tape-section-inner">

      <p class="tape-number">01 / THE URGE</p>

      <h2>
        WHAT ARE YOU<br>
        ABOUT TO DO?
      </h2>

      <div class="tape-copy">

        <p>
          Just one drink. Buy it now. Send the text.
          Do something to make this feeling stop.
          The impulse feels urgent, and your attention
          narrows to what you want right now.
        </p>

        <p>
          Notice what's happening. Are you bored,
          hurting, angry, lonely, tired, restless, ashamed, or caught in
          racing thoughts? The urge is real, but
          it isn't a command. You can want something
          without immediately acting on it.
        </p>

        <p>
          You don't have to fight the feeling or
          shame yourself for having it. Recognize
          it, get curious about it, and give
          yourself a moment before deciding.
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / THE PROMISE -->
  <section class="tape-section tape-black">
    <div class="tape-section-inner">

      <p class="tape-number">02 / THE PROMISE</p>

      <h2>
        THE PART YOUR<br>
        BRAIN KEEPS<br>
        REPLAYING.
      </h2>

      <div class="tape-copy">

        <p>
          Every impulse makes a promise. Drinking
          promises relief. Spending promises excitement.
          Sending the message promises to make
          someone understand.
        </p>

        <p>
          Don't pretend the payoff isn't appealing.
          The relief may be real. The excitement
          may feel good. But how long does it last,
          and what usually comes afterward?
        </p>

        <p>
          Ask what you actually need. Comfort?
          Rest? Pleasure? Connection? The part of
          you demanding immediate relief may
          have a legitimate need and a terrible
          idea for meeting it.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="tape-callout">
        <p>
          WANTING RELIEF MAKES SENSE.<br>
          NOT EVERY SOLUTION DOES.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / THE REST OF THE TAPE -->
  <section class="tape-section tape-yellow">
    <div class="tape-section-inner">

      <p class="tape-number">03 / THE REST OF THE TAPE</p>

      <h2>
        KEEP THE<br>
        TAPE<br>
        RUNNING.
      </h2>

      <div class="tape-copy">

        <p>
          Your brain is showing you the payoff.
          Keep watching. What happens after the
          drink, purchase, or message?
          What about tomorrow? Next week?
        </p>

        <p>
          Use your actual history instead of
          inventing the worst possible ending.
          If "just one" has turned into ten
          before, include that. If impulsive
          spending has left you short on money,
          remember that too.
        </p>

        <p>
          Consider the effects on your sobriety,
          finances, relationships, responsibilities,
          and trust in yourself. Separate what
          you know from what you're predicting.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="tape-grid">

        <div class="tape-box">
          <h3>RIGHT AFTER.</h3>
          <p>
            What do you get immediately?
            Relief, excitement, distraction?
            What might happen once that
            first feeling wears off?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="tape-box">
          <h3>TOMORROW.</h3>
          <p>
            What will you wake up to?
            Consider your mood, money,
            sobriety, relationships,
            and unfinished responsibilities.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="tape-box">
          <h3>IF THIS KEEPS GOING.</h3>
          <p>
            What happens if this becomes
            a pattern? What does it cost,
            and is the original payoff
            worth repeating those consequences?
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / THE OTHER TAPE -->
  <section class="tape-section tape-purple">
    <div class="tape-section-inner">

      <p class="tape-number">04 / THE OTHER TAPE</p>

      <h2>
        PLAY THE<br>
        OTHER TAPE.
      </h2>

      <div class="tape-copy">

        <p>
          Now imagine not acting on the impulse.
          You put down the phone, leave the store,
          close the shopping app, or drive past the liquor store.
        </p>

        <p>
          The uncomfortable feeling might not
          disappear immediately. You could still
          be bored, angry, restless, or hurting.
          But you haven't created another problem
          to deal with tomorrow.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can choose something smaller:
          eat, rest, change your surroundings,
          ask for company, follow your relapse
          plan, or talk to your psych
          about a concerning mood change.
        </p>

        <p>
          Waiting isn't doing nothing.
          Sometimes the most effective action
          is refusing to let urgency
          make the decision for you.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="tape-callout">
        <p>
          YOU CAN WANT THE THING<br>
          AND STILL CHOOSE NOT TO DO IT.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 / YOUR NEXT MOVE -->
  <section class="tape-section tape-black">
    <div class="tape-section-inner">

      <p class="tape-number">05 / YOUR NEXT MOVE</p>

      <h2>WHAT NOW?</h2>

      <div class="tape-copy">

        <p>
          You can't guarantee tomorrow's mood
          or control someone else's response.
          You can consider your next action
          and whether it reflects the life
          you're trying to build.
        </p>

        <p>
          Your circumstances matter. Mania,
          depression, pain, and exhaustion
          can affect judgment. Acknowledge
          those influences without ignoring
          the consequences of your decisions.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you've already acted on the impulse,
          the tape doesn't end there. Tell the
          truth, deal with what happened,
          repair what you can, and reconsider
          the safeguards you need.
        </p>

        <p>
          Playing the tape forward isn't about
          predicting disaster or making perfect
          choices. It's about remembering the
          scenes your brain conveniently skips
          and giving yourself enough room
          to make a deliberate decision.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="tape-callout">
        <p>
          DON'T STOP THE TAPE<br>
          AT THE PART YOU LIKE.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 / PRESS PLAY -->
  <section class="tape-section tape-pink">
    <div class="tape-section-inner">

      <p class="tape-number">06 / PRESS PLAY</p>

      <h2>
        STOP THINKING<br>
        ABOUT IT.<br>
        RUN THE TAPE.
      </h2>

      <div class="tape-copy">

        <p>
          You don't need to work through an entire
          philosophy lesson when you're about
          to make an impulsive decision.
          You need a few honest questions.
        </p>

        <p>
          Answer these before you drink,
          spend the money, send the message,
          or act on whatever suddenly
          feels absolutely necessary.
        </p>

      </div>


      <!-- QUICK EXERCISE -->
      <div class="tape-grid">

        <div class="tape-box">
          <h3>01 / WHAT DO I WANT?</h3>
          <p>
            Name the exact action you're considering.
            Not the feeling. Not the excuse.
            What are you actually about to do?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="tape-box">
          <h3>02 / WHAT'S THE PAYOFF?</h3>
          <p>
            Relief? Excitement? Escape?
            Connection? Revenge?
            What are you expecting
            this decision to give you?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="tape-box">
          <h3>03 / WHAT HAPPENS NEXT?</h3>
          <p>
            Keep the tape rolling.
            What happens in an hour?
            Tomorrow? Next week?
            Use your history, not your imagination.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="tape-box">
          <h3>04 / WHAT IF I DON'T?</h3>
          <p>
            What discomfort might you have
            to tolerate? What do you protect
            by choosing not to act?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="tape-box">
          <h3>05 / WHAT'S MY NEXT MOVE?</h3>
          <p>
            Choose one action you can take now.
            Wait ten minutes. Leave the situation.
            Put down the phone. Ask for help.
            Give yourself another option.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>


      <div class="tape-callout">
        <p>
          YOU DON'T HAVE TO ACT<br>
          JUST BECAUSE YOU FEEL READY.
        </p>
      </div>
